. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H2 *
. 
. //having a daughter, the respondent is more likely to subscribe to identify w
> ith left-leaning politics
. //we analyze the change in attitude before and after having a child within th
> e period of 2005 and 2019
. //we generate a political orientation scale from plh0004 (Political attitude 
> [2005,2009,2014,2019]) and then standardize
. //we run a OLS regression analysis with political orientation scale as depend
> ent variable at more recent wave after childbirth and the dummy variable daug
> hter as main independent variable as well as orientation scale as dependent a
> t baseline wave and dummy variable father
. //the second and third regressions are robustness checks for
. 
. gl CONTROLS1 "father edu1 edu2 edu3 edu4 edu5 child_age_t age_t year year_t"

. gl CONTROLS2 "catholic protestant nonreligious migrant married hhincome_cap h
> gfhinc fulltime parttime workhours_cap workhours_miss disability"

. gl CONTROLS3 "birth_east siblings_female siblings_male"

. 
. gl reg1 "reg polpref_tstd daughter polpref_blstd $CONTROLS1 if exclusion==0, 
> robust"

. gl reg2 "reg polpref_tstd daughter polpref_blstd $CONTROLS1 $CONTROLS2 if exc
> lusion==0, robust"

. gl reg3 "reg polpref_tstd daughter polpref_blstd $CONTROLS1 $CONTROLS2 $CONTR
> OLS3 if exclusion==0, robust"

. 
. sum polpref_*std $CONTROLS1 $CONTROLS2 $CONTROLS3 if exclusion==0

